<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cales</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cales was an ancient city of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, at modern Calvi Risorta. Originally an Ausonian town, it was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> colony there in 334 BCE on land taken from the Sidicini and Aurunci. It became known for its black-glazed pottery and its wine, and remained a town through the Roman period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cales.jpg" alt="Cales" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PietroCifone · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_0000.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
